<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zedekiah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned <a href="/years/597-bce">597</a>–587 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zedekiah was the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, placed on the throne by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 597 BCE. When he rebelled with Egyptian encouragement, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, which fell in 587 or 586 BCE. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> he was captured near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a>, forced to watch his sons killed, blinded and taken in chains to Babylon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zedekiah.jpg" alt="Zedekiah" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guillaume Rouille · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zedekiah.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zedekiah, born Mattaniah, was the twentieth and last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. The Neo-Babylonian defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> in 612 BCE began the upheavals that destroyed the kingdom. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a> tried to block the Egyptian army marching north in 608 BCE and was mortally wounded at Megiddo. His son Jehoahaz was deposed after three months by the pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii">Necho II</a> in favour of an older brother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoiakim">Jehoiakim</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> at Carchemish in 605 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Jehoiakim died during a later siege and was succeeded by his son Jeconiah, but Jerusalem fell within three months. Nebuchadnezzar deposed Jeconiah and installed his uncle Mattaniah, renamed Zedekiah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, Zedekiah was twenty-one when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> made him king in 597 BCE. A Babylonian chronicle agrees that its king captured the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> and appointed a king of his own choice. Despite the strong remonstrances of the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremiah">Jeremiah</a>, Baruch ben Neriah and others, Zedekiah allied with the Egyptian pharaoh Hophra and revolted. Nebuchadnezzar besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> for about thirty months. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-jeremiah">Book of Jeremiah</a>, Zedekiah sends to the prophet for word from God, imprisons him in his palace, questions him in secret, and refuses his officials' demand to execute him while letting them throw him into a cistern. Ebed-melech then took thirty men to lift Jeremiah out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> fell in 587 or 586 BCE; scholars remain divided between the two years. According to the biblical accounts, Zedekiah and his followers tried to escape but were captured on the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a> and taken to Riblah, where he saw his sons put to death. His eyes were then put out, and he was carried in chains to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, where he remained a prisoner until he died. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> sent Nebuzaradan to destroy Jerusalem, which was plundered and razed and its temple destroyed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> became the Babylonian province of Yehud, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedaliah">Gedaliah</a> ben Ahikam as governor; after Gedaliah was assassinated, most of the population fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zedekiah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Josiah’s death, Jehoiakim served Egypt and then Babylon, and the last kings, Jehoiachin and <b>Zedekiah</b>, were installed and deposed by foreign powers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Babylonian Chronicle records that Nebuchadnezzar took Jerusalem, carried King Jehoiachin into exile and installed <b>Zedekiah</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-16">16 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=-591" aria-label="Jerusalem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5458 1007 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zedekiah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5908 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=-591">What was here in 591 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
